#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:1
msgid "The three segments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:3
msgid "*Implemented — with a fourth that is designed and not built.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:5
msgid ""
"Each page class maps to a **bundle**: the pipelines applied to a page of "
"that class. A pipeline is three segments, in a fixed order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:8
msgid ""
"One page through a pipeline: pdf_extract, text_filter, deserialize, and "
"the promise map"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:12
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**One page through one pipeline.** The page is cut into `PdfBlock`s; the "
"blocks that concern the funds being looked for become `TextBlock`s; each "
"of those is deserialized independently into an entity or into promise "
"entries. This diagram covers the pipeline only — what decides *which* "
"pipeline a page gets is {doc}`classification` and {doc}`schedule`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:18
msgid "The three questions the segments answer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:19
msgid ""
"**`pdf_extract` — what is on this page.** Takes the page, returns "
"`PdfBlock`s. By convention it looks only at **graphical** evidence: font,"
" size, position, and text that is part of the layout rather than of the "
"content. Keeping it away from meaning is what makes it reusable — a table"
" is a table regardless of what the numbers in it are about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:24
#, python-format
msgid ""
"**`text_filter` — does any of this concern us.** Takes the blocks — *all*"
" of them, because interpreting one may depend on another — and returns "
"`TextBlock`s, each keeping a reference back to the PDF block it came "
"from. This is where the target companies enter, and where most of a run's"
" time is spent: measured, 85–96% of the engine's work, and a single "
"standard pipe inside it accounts for 30–54% of a whole job."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:30
#, python-format
msgid ""
"**`deserialize` — what do the survivors mean.** Takes each text block "
"**independently** and produces a typed entity. No filtering happens here:"
" once meaning is settled the transformation is per block, so the segment "
"is *defined* to be per block. Measured at under 0.2% of a run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:34
msgid "Why three segments, and not a graph"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:35
msgid ""
"**Rejected: a general graph of transformations**, which is more "
"expressive and would let a format do anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:38
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The three segments answer three **separable** questions, and separability"
" is the entire benefit: an author replaces one segment and inherits the "
"other two. That is what keeps a hard format from costing three times a "
"simple one, and it is what the merge across the three levels operates on "
"({doc}`formats-as-plugins`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:43
msgid ""
"A general graph gives up exactly that. Nothing then says which node an "
"author may replace in isolation, and \"inherit the rest\" stops having a "
"meaning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:46
msgid "Why there is no fourth segment yet"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:47
msgid ""
"A fourth was considered and not added, and the machinery is **generic in "
"the number of segments**, so adding one is cheap if a fourth separable "
"question ever appears."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:50
msgid ""
"None had — until one was proposed. It is described below, marked as what "
"it is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:52
msgid "The planned fourth segment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:54
msgid "Planned, not implemented"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:57
msgid ""
"Today `text_filter` does two jobs: it **filters** (does this concern the "
"companies we are looking for) and it **interprets semantically** (what do"
" these blocks mean as a unit). Those are separable, and the design to "
"separate them exists:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:61
msgid ""
"one segment for **layout interpretation** and one for **semantic "
"interpretation**, both of which an author must specify, because they are "
"what a format actually is;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:63
msgid ""
"**filtering becomes its own segment with a default that filters "
"nothing**, so a format that does not need it never writes it;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:65
msgid ""
"**`deserialize` gains a standard implementation too**, dispatching on the"
" block type to the standard constructor for that kind of value — ideally "
"by deserializing the metadata coming out of the filter, since the "
"outgoing classes are already typed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:69
msgid ""
"The shape of the win is that the two segments a format *must* describe "
"are the two that are genuinely format-specific, and the two that are "
"usually boilerplate get defaults. It is why the segment count was kept "
"generic rather than hard-coded at three."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/segments.md:73
msgid "Nothing in the engine implements this yet, and no format can use it."
msgstr ""

